syslog: reconnect when the syslogd socket refuses, not only on reset

vsyslog() reopens the log socket only on ECONNRESET. The socket is a
connected AF_UNIX datagram socket by default, and once syslogd restarts
(its /dev/log is unlinked and created again) a send on the old socket
fails with ECONNREFUSED. That falls to the default case: the socket is
closed, the message goes to the console and is lost, and only the next
call reconnects. So every program logging through syslog() loses one
line per syslogd restart. On the ODI stick, busybox klogd is one of them:
the first kernel line after a syslogd restart never reaches syslog
(observed on hardware, 2026-10-13). glibc retries a failed send once
after reconnecting, whatever the error.

Treat ECONNREFUSED and ENOTCONN like ECONNRESET, and bound the reopen
to one retry per message so a socket that keeps failing cannot loop.

Worth reporting upstream; it is a real bug, not a local quirk.

--- a/libc/misc/syslog/syslog.c
+++ b/libc/misc/syslog/syslog.c
@@ -199,6 +199,7 @@
 	time_t now;
 	int fd, saved_errno;
 	int rc;
+	int reopened = 0;
 	char tbuf[1024]; /* syslogd is unable to handle longer messages */
 
 	/* Just throw out this message if pri has bad bits. */
@@ -283,8 +284,16 @@
 				case EINTR:
 					break;
 				case ECONNRESET:
-					/* syslogd restarted, reopen log */
+				case ECONNREFUSED:
+				case ENOTCONN:
+					/* syslogd restarted, reopen log: a stream socket
+					 * reports ECONNRESET, but the datagram socket used
+					 * by default reports ECONNREFUSED once the old
+					 * syslogd socket is gone. Retry once, so a syslogd
+					 * that accepts and then refuses cannot loop here. */
 					closelog_intern(1);
+					if (reopened++)
+						goto write_err;
 					openlog_intern();
 					goto retry;
 				case EAGAIN:
